      "tip": "**বড়** (*boro*) = big, **ছোট** (*chhoto*) = small. With family they mean **elder** and **younger**: **বড় ভাই** = elder brother, **ছোট বোন** = younger sister. Bengali almost always says *which* sibling.\n\nYou don't call an elder sibling by name; say **ভাইয়া** (*bhaiya*, elder brother) or **আপু** (*apu*, elder sister). Younger siblings you *do* call by name. In Kolkata the words are দাদা and দিদি, but in Bangladesh **দাদা** means *grandfather* (next lesson).\n\n'My/your' forms of the pronouns end in **-র**: সে → **তার**, ও → **ওর**, উনি → **ওনার**, তুই → **তোর**. They don't show gender: **তার নাম** = 'his name' or 'her name'. Use ওনার for someone you'd call আপনি, and তোর only with people you'd call তুই.",
