      "tip": "English has one 'uncle'; Bengali has a different word for each one! On your **father's side**:\n\n- **দাদা / দাদি**: grandfather / grandmother\n- **চাচা / চাচি**: father's brother / his wife\n- **ফুফু / ফুফা**: father's sister / her husband (also spelled **ফুপু / ফুপা**)\n\nAdd **-র** for 'father's': বাবা → **বাবার**. So **চাচা আমার বাবার ভাই** = 'Chacha is my father's brother.' You address relatives with these words too: **চাচা, আপনি কেমন আছেন?**\n\n**Kolkata difference:** in Bangladesh **দাদা** is your *grandfather*, but in Kolkata (and many Hindu families) it means *elder brother*; there paternal grandparents are ঠাকুরদা / ঠাকুমা. This course uses the Bangladesh meaning.",
